n value, and eligible premium cards can access Points Boost listings worth up to 1.5 or 2 cents per point. However, transferring points to airline and hotel partners can achieve an average value of 2.05 cents per point based on June 2026 valuations from The Points Guy, making partner transfers the superior financial choice for high-end itineraries."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is it cheaper to use the Chase Travel portal or transfer points?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Transferring points is generally cheaper in terms of the total points required for premium airline seats and luxury hotels, while the portal is often comparable or cheaper for budget domestic travel. Because the portal values points at a fixed 1 cent baseline (or up to 1.5 to 2 cents on boosted listings), expensive cash tickets require large point sums. Airline and hotel transfer partners use award charts that can require significantly fewer points for the same itinerary, averaging 2.05 cents per point in value according to June 2026 data from The Points Guy."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the Chase Travel portal price match?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Chase is testing a hotel Price Match Guarantee, but it is currently in a limited beta for select premium cardholders only. According to reporting from Forbes Advisor, the pilot began on March 18, 2026, and is restricted to select Chase Sapphire Reserve, Chase Sapphire Reserve for Business, and J.P. Morgan Reserve accounts. It applies only to prepaid hotel bookings where a competing public rate in United States dollars matches the exact room, bed, cancellation terms, and guest count with a nightly price difference greater than $5. Flights, rental cars, and other bookings are not eligible."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do all Chase cards get the same value through the portal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, point values through the portal vary based on the specific credit card you hold and when your account was opened. All Ultimate Rewards cards receive a baseline value of 1 cent per point on standard bookings. Premium cards like the Chase Sapphire Preferred and Ink Business Preferred can redeem select Points Boost inventory at up to 1.5 cents per point, while the Chase Sapphire Reserve can redeem Points Boost listings at up to 2 cents per point. Additionally, cardholders who held eligible cards before the Points Boost overhaul applied before June 23, 2025, may retain prior baseline redemption rates during a temporary transition window, according to reporting from The Points Guy."}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/","jobTitle":"Personal Finance Writer & Editor","description":"Jonathan Velez writes and edits ModernWallet's personal-finance content, focused on loans, interest, and debt payoff. Every calculator page is reviewed against primary sources like the CFPB and Federal Reserve.","knowsAbout":["Auto loans","Loan amortization","Interest and APR","Debt payoff strategies","Personal finance"],"worksFor":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com"}}</script> <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Is the Chase Travel Portal Worth It for Your Points?","description":"Compare Chase Travel portal point values against transfer partners. Learn baseline rates, Points Boost math, and when booking through Chase makes sense.","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/guides/chase-travel-portal-worth-it/","image":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"},"datePublished":"2026-09-10","dateModified":"2026-09-10","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"}},"citation":[{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"Chase","url":"https://www.chase.com/personal/credit-cards/education/basics/how-to-use-chase-ultimate-rewards-travel-portal"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"Chase Travel","url":"https://www.chase.com/travel/guide/trips/chase-sapphire-points-boost-benefits-guide"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"The Points Guy","url":"https://thepointsguy.com/loyalty-programs/chase-ultimate-rewards-points-value"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"Forbes Advisor","url":"https://www.forbes.com/advisor/credit-cards/chase-travel-price-match-guarantee-lmandp5/"}]}</script> <main class="page" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <a href="/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>ModernWallet</a> <span data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>/</span> <a href="/guides/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Guides</a> <span data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>/</span> <span class="crumb-current" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Is the Chase Travel Portal Worth It for Your Points?</span> </nav> <header class="hero" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h1 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Is the Chase Travel Portal Worth It for Your Points?</h1> <div class="byline" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr> <span class="by" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>By <a href="/about/" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Jonathan Velez</a>, Personal Finance Writer &amp; Editor</span> <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>·</span> <span class="rev" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Reviewed against primary sources · Updated September 10, 2026</span> </div>   <p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>The Chase Travel portal is worth using for straightforward economy flights, routine hotel stays, and select Points Boost inventory. It is rarely the optimal choice for premium airline seats or luxury hotel bookings. At ModernWallet, we evaluate travel booking channels by comparing their fixed point redemption baselines against what cardholders could extract elsewhere with the same balance.</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Every card earning Chase Ultimate Rewards (UR) points can book travel at a baseline value of 1 cent per point. Select premium cards unlock rotating listings through Points Boost that elevate value up to 1.5 or 2 cents per point. In contrast, transferring those same points to airline and hotel partners can yield a benchmark valuation of 2.05 cents per point based on The Points Guy's June 2026 valuation. While <a href="/guides/chase-travel-portal-guide/">our Chase Travel portal guide</a> details the platform mechanics and booking steps, this guide analyzes the mathematical tradeoffs to help you determine when the portal earns a competitive return and when transferring your points is the smarter move.</p> </header> <section class="tools" aria-label="Tools for this journey" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <span class="tools-title" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Tools for this journey</span> <div class="tool-pills" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <a class="tool-pill" href="/budget/" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Budget<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>→</span></a> </div> </section>   <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Baseline Redemption Rates and Points Boost Math</h2> <p>Standard redemptions through the Chase portal yield a fixed baseline of 1 cent per point across every Ultimate Rewards credit card. Holding a card such as the Chase Freedom Unlimited, Chase Freedom Flex, Chase Sapphire Preferred, or Chase Sapphire Reserve establishes a floor where 10,000 points equal $100 in travel value, and 50,000 points equal $500 in travel value. At this 1-cent baseline, booking flights, hotels, rental cars, or cruises through the portal functions as cash-equivalent spending. If you are still choosing between the two Freedom cards, read <a href="/compare/chase-freedom-flex-vs-unlimited/">Chase Freedom Flex vs Unlimited</a> first.</p><p>To increase that return without transferring to outside partners, cardholders can look for listings tagged with Chase Travel Points Boost. Points Boost provides elevated redemption rates on a rotating selection of flights and hotels. On the Chase Sapphire Preferred, Ink Business Preferred, and Ink Business Plus, boosted listings can redeem for up to 1.5 cents per point. On the Chase Sapphire Reserve, Chase Sapphire Reserve for Business, and <a data-entity-link="1" href="https://www.jpmorgan.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">J.P. Morgan</a> Reserve, boosted listings can reach up to 2 cents per point.</p><p>Account timing also changes the equation. Cardholders who held an eligible credit card before the Points Boost overhaul applied before June 23, 2025, may still redeem at their card's previous fixed rate for a designated transition window. For instance, according to valuation analysis published by <a href="https://thepointsguy.com/loyalty-programs/chase-ultimate-rewards-points-value" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Points Guy</a> (TPG) in June 2026, legacy Chase Sapphire Reserve cardholders may retain a baseline of 1.5 cents per point across all portal bookings during their transition period rather than dropping to 1 cent for non-boosted inventory. Individual account notices specify whether a transition rate remains active on your balance.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Comparing Portal Value to Airline and Hotel Transfer Partners</h2> <p>Transferring Chase Ultimate Rewards points to airline and hotel partners at a 1:1 ratio offers an average redemption value of 2.05 cents per point according to the June 2026 valuation from The Points Guy. The Points Guy establishes 2.05 cents as the standard target value for a well-executed transfer-partner redemption. When comparing this 2.05-cent figure against the portal baseline of 1 cent per point, transferring points produces more than double the purchasing power for the exact same point balance.</p><p>Even when compared against the 1.5-cent cap on Sapphire Preferred Points Boost listings or the 2-cent cap on Sapphire Reserve Points Boost listings, the 2.05-cent transfer benchmark remains higher. A balance of 50,000 Ultimate Rewards points delivers $500 in travel through a baseline 1-cent portal booking. That same 50,000-point balance redeemed through a boosted listing at 1.5 cents per point yields $750 in value, and at 2 cents per point yields $1,000 in value.</p><p>When transferred to a partner program and redeemed at the 2.05-cent target valuation, those 50,000 points deliver $1,025 in travel value. Cardholders weighing programs such as <a href="/compare/bilt-rewards-vs-chase-ultimate-rewards/">Bilt Rewards vs Chase Ultimate Rewards</a> often evaluate this exact dynamic. Transferable point currencies carry their highest potential value when moved to partner loyalty charts rather than spent inside proprietary cash portals.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Circumstances Where the Chase Travel Portal Is Worth It for Simplicity</h2> <p>Booking directly through the Chase Travel portal is the better decision when reserving domestic economy flights, independent hotel properties, or itineraries with zero partner award availability. Partner loyalty programs operate award charts that require specific award seat or room availability. On high-demand dates, peak holidays, or regional domestic routes, airlines and hotels frequently block award inventory or impose heavy dynamic surcharges. The Chase Travel portal avoids this bottleneck entirely.</p><p>If a seat or hotel room is available for cash purchase, it is bookable with points through the portal. Flights booked through the portal are treated as commercial cash tickets by airlines. This means you generally earn airline frequent flyer miles and elite status credits on the ticket, which award flights transferred to partners do not provide.</p><p>If your search surfaces a Points Boost hotel or flight listing that prices out at 1.5 cents or 2 cents per point, you capture a redemption value that approaches the transfer target without the friction of learning partner award charts. For travelers balancing a monthly spending plan using our <a href="/budget/">budget calculator</a>, the certainty of immediate cash-equivalent booking often outweighs the effort required to search multiple airline award engines.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Redemptions Where the Chase Travel Portal Is Rarely Worth It</h2> <p>Transferring points to airline and hotel partners significantly outperforms the Chase Travel portal when booking premium cabin international flights and luxury hotel stays. The underlying reason stems from how cash prices scale compared to award charts. The Chase Travel portal ties the point cost of a reservation directly to its cash price.</p><p>At the 1-cent baseline rate, a ticket with a high cash price requires a massive number of points. For every $1,000 in cash cost, a traveler must spend 100,000 Ultimate Rewards points through the portal. Even with Points Boost on a Sapphire Reserve, that same $1,000 cost consumes 50,000 points at 2 cents per point.</p><p>In contrast, airline and hotel loyalty programs price awards using proprietary charts or separate award brackets. When a partner program prices an expensive international seat or luxury room at a competitive mileage rate, transferring points allows the cardholder to achieve or exceed the 2.05-cent benchmark valuation from The Points Guy. Spending points in the portal at 1 cent per point on high-dollar itineraries wastes balance efficiency, effectively cutting your point value in half relative to a well-timed partner transfer.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>The Hotel Price Match Guarantee Pilot and Eligibility Rules</h2> <p>Chase is testing a Price Match Guarantee for prepaid hotel bookings made through Chase Travel, but the beta pilot currently applies to a strictly limited group of premium cardholders. According to reporting from <a href="https://www.forbes.com/advisor/credit-cards/chase-travel-price-match-guarantee-lmandp5/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Forbes Advisor</a>, Chase launched this beta feature on March 18, 2026. The pilot is limited exclusively to select cardholders with the Chase Sapphire Reserve, Chase Sapphire Reserve for Business, and J.P. Morgan Reserve.</p><p>Chase has indicated plans to expand the feature to additional Sapphire Reserve cardholders over time, but the bank has not announced a specific rollout schedule or confirmed whether it will ever reach other cards such as the Chase Sapphire Preferred or Ink business cards. The Price Match Guarantee applies solely to prepaid hotel stays; flights, rental cars, and cruises are completely excluded. To submit a claim, cardholders must contact Chase Travel within 24 hours of booking.</p><p>The competing rate must be publicly available to view and book in United States dollars (USD). It must match the exact room type, bed type, cancellation policy, and number of guests, with a nightly rate difference greater than $5. Rates restricted to loyalty program members, corporate codes, coupons, or special promotional deals do not qualify. When Chase approves a price match claim, the difference is credited back to the original payment method within 1 to 2 billing cycles.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>When to Skip the Chase Travel Portal</h2> <p>Our verdict on the portal shifts with two specific policy changes, covered below.</p><p>The Chase Travel portal is the wrong tool for travelers pursuing elite hotel loyalty benefits and those who prioritize international first or business class travel. Major hotel chains rarely grant elite status nights, points earning, or on-property elite perks on reservations booked through third-party travel agencies like Chase Travel. If you hold hotel elite status with any major chain, booking through the portal sacrifices those elite-night credits and on-property perks. Travelers in that category should transfer points directly to partner hotel programs or book directly with the property.</p><p>Similarly, travelers looking to extract maximum value from the <a href="/roundup/best-travel-credit-cards/">best travel credit cards</a> on long-haul premium cabins should skip the portal and transfer directly to airline partners. Our evaluation would shift if Chase introduced a universal 1.5-cent baseline value across all redemptions on premium cards, eliminated the restrictions on Points Boost inventory, or broadened the hotel price match policy to every cardholder. Conversely, if airline and hotel transfer partners enacted sweeping devaluations to their award charts that pulled average transfer values down toward 1 cent per point, the portal would become the undisputed recommendation for all bookings.</p><p>To decide if the Chase Travel portal is worth it for your itinerary, calculate the cent-per-point return against partner transfer options before you redeem.</p> </section>  <section class="section" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <h2 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="faqs" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>How much are Chase Ultimate Rewards points worth in dollars?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>At the baseline value of 1 cent per point, 100 Chase Ultimate Rewards points equal $1.00, meaning 10,000 points are worth $100, 50,000 points are worth $500, and 100,000 points are worth $1,000. On cards eligible for Points Boost, 50,000 points can reach $750 at 1.5 cents each or $1,000 at 2 cents each, while The Points Guy's June 2026 transfer partner benchmark of 2.05 cents values that same 50,000-point balance at $1,025.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Is the Chase Travel portal worth it?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Yes, the Chase Travel portal is worth using for standard economy flights, independent hotels, and select Points Boost listings, but it is rarely the best choice for premium international travel. Every card earns at least 1 cent per point in value, and eligible premium cards can access Points Boost listings worth up to 1.5 or 2 cents per point. However, transferring points to airline and hotel partners can achieve an average value of 2.05 cents per point based on June 2026 valuations from The Points Guy, making partner transfers the superior financial choice for high-end itineraries.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Is it cheaper to use the Chase Travel portal or transfer points?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Transferring points is generally cheaper in terms of the total points required for premium airline seats and luxury hotels, while the portal is often comparable or cheaper for budget domestic travel. Because the portal values points at a fixed 1 cent baseline (or up to 1.5 to 2 cents on boosted listings), expensive cash tickets require large point sums. Airline and hotel transfer partners use award charts that can require significantly fewer points for the same itinerary, averaging 2.05 cents per point in value according to June 2026 data from The Points Guy.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Does the Chase Travel portal price match?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Chase is testing a hotel Price Match Guarantee, but it is currently in a limited beta for select premium cardholders only. According to reporting from Forbes Advisor, the pilot began on March 18, 2026, and is restricted to select Chase Sapphire Reserve, Chase Sapphire Reserve for Business, and J.P. Morgan Reserve accounts. It applies only to prepaid hotel bookings where a competing public rate in United States dollars matches the exact room, bed, cancellation terms, and guest count with a nightly price difference greater than $5. Flights, rental cars, and other bookings are not eligible.</p> </details><details class="faq" data-astro-cid-2u677oxp> <summary data-astro-cid-2u677oxp><h3 data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>Do all Chase cards get the same value through the portal?</h3></summary> <p data-astro-cid-2u677oxp>No, point values through the portal vary based on the specific credit card you hold and when your account was opened. All Ultimate Rewards cards receive a baseline value of 1 cent per point on standard bookings. Premium cards like the Chase Sapphire Preferred and Ink Business Preferred can redeem select Points Boost inventory at up to 1.5 cents per point, while the Chase Sapphire Reserve can redeem Points Boost listings at up to 2 cents per point. Additionally, cardholders who held eligible cards before the Points Boost overhaul applied before June 23, 2025, may retain prior baseline redemption rates during a temporary transition window, according to reporting from The Points Guy.</p> </details> </div> </section>  <section class="section sources" data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x><h2 data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x>Sources</h2><p class="source-note" data-astro-cid-3ntd4h4x>
